        "reviewed_sha": "e8e0f7afb04ef6a42439ea72fdbde5a8407ba33e",
        "evidence": "No superlative language; used here as the defect channel.",
        "notes": "STRUCTURAL FINDINGS (report only). (1) The block locates the obstruction in two incompatible places ON ITS OWN PAGE: the bullet list says 'HH^2(Ad): obstructions to extending deformations across q = 1' while two paragraphs later the cup-product sentence puts the obstruction to phi_2 in HH^3. Standard Gerstenhaber theory has deformations in HH^2 and obstructions in HH^3, which is the bullet list's reading, not def:classical-limit-obstruction's Obs := HH^2. The Lean sibling classical-limit-obstruction.lean carries the same HH^2 bullet in its docstring, so the tension is corpus-wide rather than local to this block. (2) 'The total obstruction ... is the formal sum obs_infty = sum (q-1)^n [phi_n] in HH^2(Ad)[[q-1]]. This formal power series CONVERGES iff all Massey products vanish through the tower.' A formal power series in R[[q-1]] has no convergence notion, and Massey-product vanishing is a statement about the obstruction sequence, not about convergence - as written the sentence is a category error. Proposed: 'the deformation is unobstructed to all orders iff all Massey products vanish'. (3) The iff 'A property P(q) persists through q = 1 if and only if its obstruction class [P] vanishes' is asserted as fact, but in the Lean it is the ASSUMED structure field extends_iff_zero - an axiomatisation, not a theorem (AGENTS.md 'Axiomatization is NOT a Proof'). (4) Minor index mismatch: the tower is indexed n >= 1 ([phi_n], kappa_n) but the last sentence computes it via kappa_0 = e(xi)."
      }
    ],
